Seek with a 64-bit offset in fseeko.
fseeko was defined as fseek, whose offset is a long, which has 32 bits
on Windows even in 64-bit builds, so the 64-bit offset was truncated.
A seek of 2 GiB or more into a file either failed, when the low 32
bits were negative, or went to the wrong place. This broke od -j and
hexdump -s on large files, which printed data from the wrong offset
under addresses for the right one, and the seeks diff makes to reread
lines of large files. Define fseeko as fseeko64, which takes a 64-bit
offset.

---
 include/mingw.h |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/include/mingw.h
+++ b/include/mingw.h
@@ -140,7 +140,7 @@
  * stdio.h
  */
 #undef fseeko
-#define fseeko(f,o,w) fseek(f,o,w)
+#define fseeko(f,o,w) fseeko64(f,o,w)
 
 int fdprintf(int fd, const char *format, ...);
 FILE* mingw_fopen(const char *filename, const char *mode) FAST_FUNC;
